.TH SSHH 1 2026-10-17 "sshh 0.9.2" "User Commands"
.\" Keep in sync with the CLI (src/cli.rs), the TUI shortcuts (src/tui) and README.md.
.SH NAME
sshh \- SSH connection manager with a terminal UI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY sshh
.OP ssh-options
.I destination
.RI [ command " [" argument ...]]
.YS
.SY sshh
.YS
.SY sshh
.I subcommand
.RI [ options ]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sshh
keeps a database of SSH connections with a name, description, tags and
notes, and lets you use them from a terminal UI or directly from the
command line, just like
.BR ssh (1).
.PP
It never reimplements SSH: every connection runs the system
.BR ssh ,
so
.BR ssh_config (5),
.BR ssh-agent (1),
.BR ProxyJump ,
.BR ControlMaster ,
known_hosts and hardware keys keep working.
.B sshh
doesn't store passwords or keys.
.PP
It works in three ways:
.TP
.B Wrapper mode
.B sshh
.I destination
accepts the same arguments as
.BR ssh .
See
.BR "WRAPPER MODE" .
.TP
.B Terminal UI
.B sshh
without arguments opens the TUI: a connection list with fuzzy search and
details, and embedded ssh sessions in columns next to it.
See
.BR "TERMINAL UI" .
If standard output is not a terminal, it prints the list instead.
.TP
.B Subcommands
For scripts and quick changes.
See
.BR SUBCOMMANDS .
.SH WRAPPER MODE
.B sshh
parses the ssh command line to find the destination
.RI ( [user@]host
or
.IR ssh://[user@]host[:port] )
and then always runs
.B ssh
with
.BR exec (3).
Depending on the destination:
.TP
.B The alias of a saved connection
Its options are added before the destination as
.BI \-o " Key=Value"
.RB ( HostName ,
.BR User ,
.BR Port ,
.BR IdentityFile ,
.B ProxyJump
and any extra option), but only those you didn't set yourself:
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-i ,
.B \-J
and
.B \-o
on the command line always win.
.TP
.B A target matching a saved connection
When
.I user@hostname:port
is the same as a saved connection, the arguments are passed untouched and
the connection is recorded in the history.
.B sshh
asks
.B "ssh \-G"
where ssh would really connect, so aliases from
.I ~/.ssh/config
are recognised too.
.TP
.B A destination that isn't saved
In an interactive terminal, a wizard offers to save it before connecting
(see
.BR WIZARD ).
.TP
.B No destination, or unknown arguments
Everything is passed to
.B ssh
unchanged (for example
.B "sshh \-V"
or
.BR "sshh \-Q cipher" ).
.PP
Calls that only query
.RB ( \-G ,
.BR \-V ,
.BR \-O ,
.BR \-Q )
never open the wizard and aren't recorded in the history.
If something of its own fails (database, parser),
.B sshh
prints a warning and runs
.B ssh
with the original arguments.
.PP
To connect to a host named like a subcommand, use
.BR "sshh \-\- " \fIhost\fR.
.SH WIZARD
When the destination is not saved, a form prefilled from
.B "ssh \-G"
(host, user, port, ProxyJump) shows up.
The suggested alias is the short host name.
.TP
.B Enter
Save the connection and connect.
.TP
.B Esc
Connect without saving.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-n
Connect without saving and never ask again for this
.IR user @ hostname : port
(as resolved by
.BR "ssh \-G" ).
See
.BR "sshh ignore" .
.TP
.B Ctrl\-c
Cancel; don't connect.
.PP
It only appears when standard input, output and error are terminals, and
can be disabled with
.BR SSHH_NO_WIZARD=1 .
.SH TERMINAL UI
The left side shows a search box, the connection list (alias, name and
tags) and the details of the selected connection.
A green dot marks connections with a live session; a grey circle, a
session that ended.
Otherwise, next to it is the time since the last connection
(5m, 2d, 3w, 1M, 1y: minutes, hours, days, weeks, months, years).
The right side shows
.I terminal columns
side by side, each with the embedded ssh session of one connection, or an
overview of the selected connection when no column is open.
The left side takes a third of the width and gets narrower as columns are
added, down to 24% of the width (24 to 40 characters).
Under 100 columns the layout goes vertical: the search box, then the list
and the details side by side, then the panes one under the other (as many
as fit, at least 6 lines each).
The panes also go one under the other when the columns don't fit side by
side; then
.BR Alt\-j / Alt\-k
go through every pane and
.B Alt\-h
goes back to the list.
.PP
Each embedded session is the system
.B ssh
running in a pseudo terminal, so password prompts, host key questions and
full\-screen programs work as in a normal terminal.
.B sshh
answers the queries programs send to their terminal (device attributes,
cursor position, modes), and programs that ask for the mouse receive the
clicks, drags and wheel events over their column.
Sessions follow the size of their column, keep 5000 lines of history
(mouse wheel, or history mode) and accept pastes.
Quitting
.B sshh
closes them, after asking.
.SS Columns and panes
A column can hold several panes stacked vertically.
These keys work from anywhere, also while typing in a session.
On macOS, Alt is the Option key: most terminals use it to type special
characters unless it is set to act as Alt (Meta) in their settings, e.g.
"Use Option as Meta key" in Terminal.app, "Left Option key: Esc+" in iTerm2,
or
.B macos\-option\-as\-alt
in Ghostty.
.TP
.B Space
(in the list) Show the selected connection in the active pane, opening its
session.
If it is already in a pane, focus that pane; if its session ended,
reconnect.
.TP
.B Alt\-v
Show the selected connection in a new column right of the active one.
Each column needs at least 40 characters; up to 9 columns.
.TP
.B Alt\-\-
Show the selected connection in a new pane below the last column.
Each pane needs at least 6 lines.
.TP
.BR Alt\-h ", " Alt\-j ", " Alt\-k ", " Alt\-l
(or Alt and the arrows) Focus the pane on the left, below, above or on the
right.
The list is the leftmost column; there,
.B Alt\-j
and
.B Alt\-k
select the next or previous connection with a session.
.TP
.BR Alt\-1 " .. " Alt\-9
Jump to column
.IR N .
.TP
.B Alt\-J
(Alt\-Shift\-j) Stack the active pane below the column on its right, or on
its left if it is the last one.
.TP
.B Alt\-K
(Alt\-Shift\-k) Take the active pane out of its stack into its own column.
.TP
.BR Alt\-H ", " Alt\-L
(Alt\-Shift\-h, Alt\-Shift\-l) Move the active column left or right.
.TP
.BR Alt\-n ", " Alt\-p
Show the next or previous session in the active pane, skipping sessions
visible in other panes.
.TP
.B Alt\-w
Close the active pane; its neighbours take the space.
The session keeps running.
.TP
.BR Alt\-f ", " Alt\-z
Toggle full screen for the active pane, inside
.BR sshh .
.TP
.B Alt\-a
Toggle broadcast: what is typed or pasted in the active pane also goes to
every other visible pane with a live session of the same kind.
Those panes get a red border and
.B BROADCAST
in their title, and the footer shows a badge.
Sessions without a pane, ended ones and panes in history mode are left out.
It stops by itself when the last pane is closed.
.TP
.B Alt\-s
History mode for the active pane: move with
.BR j / k ,
.BR Ctrl\-d / Ctrl\-u ,
.BR gg / G ;
search backwards with
.BI / text
and
.BR n / N ;
select lines with
.BR v ;
copy the selection (or the current line) with
.B y
or
.BR Enter ;
leave with
.B q
or
.BR Esc .
.PP
While a pane has focus, every other key goes to its session,
including
.BR Esc ,
.BR Ctrl\-c ,
.B Tab
and
.BR Ctrl\-w .
Note that
.B Alt\-f
and
.B Alt\-Left/Right
don't reach the remote shell; use
.B Ctrl\-Left/Right
there to move by words.
When a session has ended,
.B Enter
or
.B Space
reconnects and
.B Esc
goes back to the list.
.SS List
.TP
.BR j ", " k ", " Down ", " Up
Move.
.TP
.BR gg ", " G
Go to the top or the bottom.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-d ", " Ctrl\-u
Half a page down or up.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-f ", " Ctrl\-b ", " PageDown ", " PageUp
A page down or up.
.TP
.BR Tab ", " Shift\-Tab
Next or previous pane: list, details, active column.
.TP
.B /
Search.
The search is fuzzy over alias, name, user, host, description and tags;
words starting with
.B #
filter by tag (prefix match).
The list is filtered while typing.
While searching,
.BR Up / Down ,
.BR Ctrl\-j / Ctrl\-k
and
.BR Ctrl\-n / Ctrl\-p
move,
.B Ctrl\-w
deletes a word,
.B Ctrl\-u
clears it, and
.B Enter
or
.B Esc
go back to the list keeping the filter.
.TP
.BR [ ", " ]
Switch the list to the previous or next tab:
.BR Connections ,
.B Tags
and
.BR Layouts .
The Tags tab lists every tag with its number of connections, and the
details pane previews the connections of the highlighted tag.
There,
.B Space
or
.B Enter
goes back to the connections filtered by that tag, and
.B Esc
goes back without filtering.
.B Alt\-v
opens every connection of the tag in panes, replacing the current ones
(their sessions keep running): as many columns as fit, the rest stacked.
Connections that don't fit are not opened.
.IP
The Layouts tab lists the saved layouts: which connection is in which pane.
There,
.B a
saves the current panes under the name you type (an existing name replaces
that layout),
.B Space
or
.B Enter
opens the highlighted layout, replacing the current panes (their sessions
keep running) and opening the sessions that aren't alive,
.B r
renames it (not to the name of another layout), and
.B dd
deletes it after confirming.
The panes of a deleted connection disappear from the layouts.
If the window is too small for the saved shape, the panes are rearranged
in a grid and those that don't fit are not opened.
Layouts are not part of
.BR "sshh export" .
.TP
.B a
Add a connection.
.TP
.BR Enter ", " e ", " t
Edit the selected connection, or edit its tags.
.TP
.B dd
Delete the selected connection (asks for confirmation).
.TP
.B yy
Copy the equivalent
.B ssh
command to the clipboard.
.TP
.B x
Close the selected connection's sessions, ssh and sftp (asks if one is
alive).
.TP
.B f
Run a full\-screen
.BR ssh ;
.B sshh
comes back when it exits.
.TP
.B s
Open
.BR sftp (1)
in a new column, next to the connection's ssh session.
.TP
.B c
Install your public key with
.BR ssh\-copy\-id (1)
(full screen;
.B sshh
comes back when it exits).
.TP
.B o
Change the sort order: recent, most used, alphabetical.
.TP
.B R
Reload the list.
.TP
.B T
Choose the theme (see
.BR THEMES ):
moving shows it,
.B Enter
saves it in the config file,
.B Esc
goes back to the previous one.
.TP
.B ?
Show the help.
.BR j / k ,
.BR Ctrl\-d / Ctrl\-u ,
.BR gg / G
and the mouse wheel scroll it;
.B /
searches it (fuzzy, matches highlighted) and
.BR n / N
go to the next or previous match.
.B Esc
clears the search, then closes the help, like
.B q
and
.BR ? .
.TP
.B Esc
Go back one level or clear the search.
It never quits.
.TP
.BR q ", " Ctrl\-c
Quit (asks first if there are live sessions).
.SS Mouse
Click selects a connection; a double click opens it.
Clicking a column focuses it; dragging over it selects its text, which is
copied to the clipboard when the button is released.
The wheel moves through the list, scrolls the details or scrolls back the
history of the column under the pointer.
Programs that ask for the mouse receive the events over their column.
Hold
.B Shift
to select text with the terminal's native selection.
.SS Form
The same form adds, edits and saves new connections in the wizard.
.B Alias
(unique, no spaces) and
.B Host
are required.
Tags are separated by commas or spaces; extra options go one per line as
.IR Key=Value .
.B tmux
is the remote tmux session to attach to (see
.BR "PERSISTENT SESSIONS" ).
.TP
.BR Tab ", " Shift\-Tab ", " Down ", " Up
Next or previous field (in multiline fields the arrows move between
lines).
.TP
.B Enter
Save (inserts a line break in Options and Notes).
.TP
.B Ctrl\-s
Save from any field.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-e
Edit Options or Notes in
.B $VISUAL
or
.BR $EDITOR .
.TP
.B Esc
Cancel (in the wizard: connect without saving).
.SH PERSISTENT SESSIONS
Sessions live inside
.BR sshh :
closing it, or losing the network, ends them.
A connection with a
.B tmux
session name runs, in interactive connections,
.PP
.RS
.B "ssh \-t" ... \fIalias\fR \(aqtmux new\-session \-A \-s \fIname\fR || exec $SHELL \-l\(aq
.RE
.PP
which attaches to that remote tmux session, creating it the first time, so
the shell survives
.B sshh
and reopening the connection (or a layout) returns to it.
tmux keeps one server per remote user: sessions only clash when several
people log in as the same user, so use a name of your own.
It applies to embedded sessions,
.BR f ,
.B yy
and
.BI "sshh " alias
in a terminal, but not with a remote command,
.BR \-N ,
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-W ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-f ,
a
.B RemoteCommand
option or without a terminal, nor to sftp or the generated ssh_config file.
Without tmux on the server, a login shell is opened instead.
Scrollback is tmux's, not history mode.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B ls
List the saved connections.
.TP
.BI add " \fR[\fPalias\fR] [\fPdestination\fR] [\fPoptions\fR]\fP"
Save a connection.
.I destination
is
.I [user@]host
or
.IR ssh://[user@]host[:port] .
Without a destination it opens the add form of the TUI, prefilled with the
alias and options given (it needs a terminal); Enter saves and Esc cancels.
Options:
.BI \-p " port" ,
.BI \-i " identity" ,
.BI \-J " jump" ,
.BI \-\-tmux " name"
(see
.BR "PERSISTENT SESSIONS" ),
.BI \-o " Key=Value"
(repeatable),
.BI \-n " name" ,
.BI \-d " description" ,
.BI \-\-notes " text" ,
.BI \-t " tags"
(comma separated, repeatable).
.TP
.BI rm " alias"
Delete a connection.
.TP
.BR history " [" \fIalias\fR "] [" "\-n \fIN\fR" ]
Show the latest connections (20 by default), of all connections or of one.
.TP
.BR export " [" "\-o \fIfile\fR" ]
Export the connections to JSON, without history.
Files are created with mode 0600.
.TP
.BI import " file"
Import connections from a JSON file created by
.B export
(or a plain list of connections);
.B \-
reads standard input.
.TP
.BR import\-ssh\-config " [" \fIfile\fR "] [" "\-t \fItags\fR" ]
Import the
.B Host
blocks with concrete names from
.I ~/.ssh/config
(or
.IR file ),
following
.B Include
directives.
Wildcard hosts,
.B Match
blocks and global options are not imported.
Comments right above a
.B Host
become its notes.
.TP
.BR ssh\-config " [" status | sync | print | disable ]
Manage
.IR ~/.ssh/config.d/sshh.conf ,
a generated
.BR ssh_config (5)
file with one
.B Host
block per connection, so that
.BR ssh ,
.BR scp ,
.BR rsync ,
.B git
and other tools know the aliases.
.B sync
writes it and keeps it updated after every change;
.B status
(the default) tells whether it is enabled and whether
.I ~/.ssh/config
includes it;
.B print
shows what would be written;
.B disable
deletes it.
.B sshh
never modifies
.IR ~/.ssh/config :
add this line at the top of it yourself, before any
.B Host
or
.B Match
block:
.RS
.PP
.EX
Include config.d/sshh.conf
.EE
.RE
.TP
.BR ignore " [" list | "add \fIdestination\fR" | "rm \fIdestination\fR" ]
List the destinations the wizard never asks to save (added with
.B Ctrl\-n
in the wizard), ignore one
.RI ( [user@]host 
.RI "or " user@host:port ,
an ssh_config alias or
.BR ssh:// \&...,
resolved with
.BR "ssh \-G" ),
or ask again for one
.RI ( user@hostname:port
as listed, or any form
.B add
accepts).
Saving a destination also removes it from the list.
.TP
.BR themes " [" list | "show \fIname\fR" ]
List the color themes, marking the one in use, or print the file of one
(to start your own from it).
See
.BR THEMES .
.SS Import options
.B import
and
.B import\-ssh\-config
accept:
.TP
.B \-\-on\-conflict skip\fR|\fBoverwrite\fR|\fBrename
What to do when a connection with the same alias and different data
exists: skip it (default), replace it, or import it as
.IR alias\-2 ,
.IR alias\-3 ...
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be imported without saving anything.
.SH THEMES
The default theme,
.BR terminal ,
leaves the colors to the terminal palette.
Any other theme sets the background, the foreground and the 16 ANSI colors
of the whole screen, the panes included; programs that use 256 colors or
truecolor keep theirs.
Built-in themes:
.BR gruvbox\-dark ", " gruvbox\-light
(palette by Pavel Pertsev),
.BR everforest\-dark ", " everforest\-light
(sainnhe),
.BR kanagawa\-wave ", " kanagawa\-dragon ", " kanagawa\-lotus
(Tommaso Laurenzi),
.B monokai
(Wimer Hazenberg),
.BR material ", " material\-palenight
(Mattia Astorino).
.PP
The theme in use is
.BR SSHH_THEME ,
or else
.B theme = \(dq\fIname\fB\(dq
in
.IR ~/.config/sshh/config.toml ,
which
.B T
in the TUI writes.
User themes are TOML files in
.IR ~/.config/sshh/themes/name.toml ,
and replace a built-in with the same name:
.B background
and
.B foreground
(\(dq#rrggbb\(dq),
.B palette
(the 16 ANSI colors) and an optional
.B [ui]
table with
.BR accent ", " match ", " tag ", " connected " and " danger .
Without
.B COLORTERM=truecolor
(or
.BR 24bit )
the nearest of the 256 colors is used.
A theme that can't be loaded falls back to
.BR terminal .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SSHH_DEBUG
When set (and not
.BR 0 ),
print to standard error how the destination is resolved and the exact
command that runs.
.TP
.B SSHH_NO_WIZARD
When set (and not
.BR 0 ),
never open the wizard for new destinations.
.TP
.B SSHH_DB
Path of the database to use.
.TP
.B SSHH_SSH
Program to run instead of the
.B ssh
found in
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B SSHH_INCLUDE_FILE
Path of the generated ssh_config file.
.TP
.B SSHH_THEME
Theme to use, over the one in the config file.
.TP
.B COLORTERM
.B truecolor
or
.B 24bit
when the terminal shows RGB colors; otherwise themes use the 256 colors.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base of the config directory (default
.IR ~/.config ).
.TP
.BR VISUAL ", " EDITOR
Editor used by
.B Ctrl\-e
in the form (default
.BR vi ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/sshh/sshh.db
SQLite database with the connections, tags and history
.RI ( $XDG_DATA_HOME/sshh/sshh.db
if set; the same path on macOS).
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/config.d/sshh.conf
Generated ssh_config file, only when enabled with
.BR "sshh ssh\-config sync" .
.TP
.I ~/.config/sshh/config.toml
Settings: the theme
.RI ( $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sshh/config.toml
if set; the same path on macOS).
.TP
.I ~/.config/sshh/themes/
User themes.
.SH EXIT STATUS
In wrapper mode the exit status is the one of
.BR ssh .
Errors of
.B sshh
itself exit with 255, like
.BR ssh .
.SH EXAMPLES
Connect, offering to save the connection if it is new:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sshh root@203.0.113.9
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save a connection and use it with a remote command and a tunnel:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sshh add web deploy@10.0.0.5 \-p 2222 \-n "Web production" \-t prod,web
sshh web uptime
sshh \-L 8080:localhost:80 web
.EE
.RE
.PP
Import the hosts of
.I ~/.ssh/config
after checking what would happen:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sshh import\-ssh\-config \-\-dry\-run
sshh import\-ssh\-config \-t ssh\-config
.EE
.RE
.PP
See how a destination is resolved, without connecting:
.PP
.RS
.EX
SSHH_DEBUG=1 sshh \-G web
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5),
.BR sftp (1),
.BR ssh\-copy\-id (1),
.BR ssh\-agent (1)
